.TH VT 1
.SH NAME
vt \- emulate an xterm
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vt
[
.B -2abcx
]
[
.B -f
.I font
] [
.B -l
.I log
] [
.I cmd
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Vt
replaces a
.I rio
window with a fresh instance of the shell,
.IR rc (1),
running within an emulation of an xterm, by
.IR vt (2).
It is for programs on other systems, reached by
.IR ssh (1)
or
.IR cpu (1),
that draw on a terminal.
If
.I cmd
is given, it is typed to the shell, as if followed by a newline.
To exit
.IR vt ,
exit the
.B rc
it starts.
.PP
.I Vt
sets
.B $TERM
to
.BR xterm-256color ,
and
.BR $LINES ,
.BR $COLS ,
.BR $COLUMNS ,
.B $XPIXELS
and
.B $YPIXELS
to the window's size, again when it changes.
A program's title is the window's label.
.PP
Lines that leave the top of the screen are kept, the last 2000.
The wheel moves through them, or, when a program asks for the mouse,
button 1 and the wheel go to it.
.SS Options
.TF m
.TP
.B 2
.TP
.B a
.TP
.B x
set
.B $TERM
to
.BR vt220 ,
.B ansi
or
.B xterm
for a host that has no
.BR xterm-256color .
.TP
.B b
changes the color scheme to white text on a black background,
but potentially with colors from escape sequences.
.TP
.B c
changes the color scheme to monochrome (no colors).
.TP
.B f
sets the
.IR font ,
best one of fixed width.
.TP
.B l
names a
.I log
file for the session, which gets what the host sends.
.SS "Raw and cooked"
In cooked mode, the default,
.I vt
keeps the line being typed and sends it whole: Backspace, control-U and control-W edit it,
control-D at its start sends an end of file, Delete interrupts the shell's command,
and Page Up and Page Down move through the kept lines.
In raw mode each key is sent as an xterm sends it, and a newline as a return.
A program asks for raw mode by writing
.B rawon
to
.BR /dev/consctl ,
as on a Plan 9 terminal; it lasts until
.B rawoff
or until the program closes the file.
.SS Menus
The right button has a menu with the following entries to provide
the sort of character processing expected by non-Plan 9 systems:
.TF cooked
.TP
.B 24x80
Resize the
.I vt
window to hold 24 rows of 80 columns.
.TP
.B crnl
Print a newline (linefeed) character after receiving a carriage return from the host.
.TP
.B cr
Do not print a newline after carriage return.
.TP
.B nlcr
Print a carriage return after receiving a newline from the host.
.TP
.B nl
Do not print a carriage return after newline.
.TP
.B raw
Enter raw mode.
.TP
.B cooked
Leave raw mode.
.TP
.B exit
Exit
.IR vt .
.PD
.PP
Each mode has its own settings: cooked mode prints a carriage return after a newline,
as Plan 9's programs need, and raw mode does not, as a Unix host's terminal does that itself.
.PP
The middle button has a menu with the following entries:
.TF right_key
.TP
.B backup
Move the display back one screenful.
.TP
.B forward
Move the display forward one screenful.
.TP
.B reset
Display the screen.
.TP
.B clear
Move the lines above the cursor's into the kept lines.
.TP
.B send
Send the contents of the
.B rio
snarf buffer, just as
.B send
in the
.B rio
menu, bracketed if the program asked for that.
.TP
.B scroll
Make new lines visible as they appear at the bottom.
.TP
.B page
When the page fills, pause and wait for a character to be typed before proceeding.
The down arrow key advances a page without sending the character to the host.
.PD
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/vt
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR vt (2)
.SH BUGS
A program on a Unix host that sets its terminal raw does not tell
.IR vt ;
choose
.B raw
from the menu.
.PP
The host is not told when the window's size changes.
